`width` and `height` on a canvas are the coordinate space the shapes were
placed in, not the size it is shown at. `scalingMode` is what happens to the
picture when the room it is given is not that size.

This is `fit`, on the four targets that draw a canvas. Each already had a name
for all four modes, so none of them needed a new mechanism -- only a value to
read. `stretch` is in canvas_scaling_mode_stretch.txtar; the default, which
scales nothing, is in canvas_scaling_mode_center.txtar, and the contrast with
it is what shows the scaled forms are not what a canvas emitted before there
was a choice.

html scales the geometry rather than the picture: the backing store is sized
to the box the canvas was laid out in, at device resolution, and the shapes
are scaled on the way in. Resampling a small drawing up to a big one is what
made a display of hard edges come out soft. The box is what the backing store
follows, so a resize redraws.

Fyne asks a Raster's generator for a picture at the pixel size it is about to
upload, which is the one thing an Image cannot know -- so a scaled canvas is a
Raster and the shapes are rasterised at the size they are shown. The surface
outlives the drawing: Fyne calls back on every redraw, and a buffer allocated
per call is the whole image thrown away per keypress. The minimum size is a
floor rather than a size: without one the box gives a canvas nothing to scale
into, which is a readout one pixel tall.

Compose draws into the room it is laid out in, so the shapes are scaled.

gtk4 is a vector context already, so it scales with a transform. The allocated
size travels with the cairo_t: GTK hands it to the draw callback, and it used
to be discarded.

deny out/none/html/index.html `object-fit` -- the shapes are drawn at the display size, so nothing is left for the browser to resample
deny out/none/html/index.html `scalingMode=` -- the mode is read as a prop and handed to the draw helper, not written out as an attribute no element has
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `FillMode` -- a Raster regenerates at the pixel size it is asked for, so the scaling is the generator's and there is no fill mode to set

-- fit.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import draw "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

window #w {
    draw.canvas(width=200px, height=100px, scalingMode=draw.ScalingMode.fit) {
        draw.rect(x=0.0, y=0.0, w=200.0, h=100.0, style=draw.CanvasStyle{fill=#3366cc})
    }
}
